11:METHODS: T1-weighted magnetic resonance imaging data from the Autism Brain Imaging Data Exchange (ABIDE) II ( n TD = 564) were used to generate a normative model to map brain structure deviations of ABIDE I subjects ( n TD = 560, n ASD = 496). Voxel-based morphometry was used to compute gray matter volume. Non-negative matrix factorization was employed to decompose the gray matter matrix into 6 factors and weights. These weights were used for normative modeling to estimate the factor deviations. Then, clustering analysis was used to identify ASD subtypes. RESULTS: Compared with TD, ASD showed increased weights and deviations in 5 factors. Three subtypes with distinct neuroanatomical deviation patterns were identi /uniFB01 ed. ASD subtype 1 and subtype 3 showed positive deviations, whereas ASD subtype 2 showed negative deviations. Distinct clinical manifestations in social communication de /uniFB01 cits were identi /uniFB01 ed among the three subtypes.
13:CONCLUSIONS: Our /uniFB01 ndings suggest that individuals with ASD have heterogeneous deviation patterns in brain structure. The results highlight the need to test for subtypes in neuroimaging studies of ASD. This study also presents a framework for understanding neuroanatomical heterogeneity in this increasingly prevalent neurodevelopmental disorder.
29:data, rather than individual quantitative measurements. Recent work from our group identi /uniFB01 ed three subtypes of male ASD by clustering analysis based on coef /uniFB01 cients characterizing the level of structural difference components. This work found different clinical symptom severity and brain functional connectivity across subtypes (16). Our previous work matched a group of most similar healthy control subjects for each ASD participant to compute a structural difference map; as such, the generalizability and clinical utility of the approach is limited. The identi /uniFB01 cation of structural MRI features that have the potential to parse heterogeneity at the individual level is an important direction for ASD research. The purpose of the current study is to develop a parameterized, low-dimensional, and individualized metric to delineate heterogeneity of brain structure in ASD.
31:Non-negative matrix factorization (NMF) has recently emerged as a promising pathway to capture robust and reliable non-negative regional differences in high-dimensional data (17) and has been widely used in MRI analysis (16,18). Using NMF, a matrix can be decomposed into a factor matrix and a weight matrix. The gray matter (GM) map of a single subject can thus be represented as a linear combination of basis factors. The use of NMF can achieve dimension reduction and increase interpretability. Normative model, a statistical technique, is increasingly applied to map variations between cognitive, clinical, behavioral, or demographic variables and quantitative brain readouts (19 -21). In contrast to case-control analysis requiring a consistent difference at the mean level, the normative model allows for detection and mapping of abnormal structural patterns individually (21,22). The normative model is similar to growth charts used in pediatric medicine, in which a child ' s height or weight is compared with a reference population (23). The integration of NMF and normative modeling, decomposition of GM into factors and weights, and modeling the weights of factors can be used to individually measure the extent of ASD difference from typical development (TD). Additionally, this strategy provides a proof of concept for delineating the heterogeneity of ASD by identifying homogeneous subgroups based on individual structural MRI features.
33:Here, we combined NMF and normative modeling to individually parameterize neurobiological heterogeneity in participants with ASD. We hypothesized that individuals with ASD would exhibit highly heterogeneous deviation patterns from the TD cohort. We predicted that individuals with ASD could be clustered into several subgroups with distinct clinical phenotypes. To test this hypothesis, we decomposed the GM matrix of Autism Brain Imaging Data Exchange (ABIDE) II TD subjects into latent factors and corresponding weights. To characterize the functional implications of latent factors, we analyzed the relationships between factors and behavioral relevance using the NeuroSynth database (24). Next, we used an NMF backreconstruction algorithm to reconstruct ABIDE I ASD and ABIDE I TD GM matrices to obtain the weights of subjects. Then, we developed a normative model by training a Gaussian process regression (GPR) on the ABIDE II TD subjects to predict the expected weights of factors. Subsequently, we estimated the deviations of weights to characterize the differences between individuals with ASD and the normative range. Finally, we applied clustering analysis to identify ASD
